Make --listen-local actually bind the data socket to the local address.

A Sun-3 boot PROM does not learn the server's IP address from RARP, so it sends
its TFTP read request to 255.255.255.255 and takes the answer from whoever
replies.  atftpd reads the request's *destination* address out of IP_PKTINFO
(tftp_io.c uses ipi_addr, not ipi_spec_dst) and binds the per-transfer data
socket to it -- which for a broadcast request is 255.255.255.255.

The result is a transfer that starts and then dies: the first DATA block goes
out, but the client's ACK is addressed to the server's real address and is never
delivered to a socket bound to the broadcast address.  Both ends time out.

    Creating new socket: 255.255.255.255:58938
    Serving C0A80079 to 192.168.0.121:1756
    timeout block 0: retrying ...
    client (192.168.0.121) not responding

atftpd already has a --listen-local option, and the comment above this code
describes precisely this problem and says the workaround is to force the socket
onto the local address.  But since the 2011 change for
http://bugs.debian.org/613582 stopped nullifying "to", the option only sets
SO_BROADCAST and no longer rebinds -- and that same comment notes SO_BROADCAST
alone does not help.  So --listen-local has quietly done nothing about the case
it was added for.

This restores the rebind, but only under --listen-local, leaving the default
path (and the behaviour 613582 asked for) untouched.  Binding to the wildcard
address lets the kernel choose the source address per route, and the socket then
receives ACKs sent to the server's real address.

Verified with tools/tftp-bcast-probe.py, which broadcasts a read request the way
the PROM does: stock atftpd stalls after block 1, patched atftpd completes.

--- a/tftpd.c
+++ b/tftpd.c
@@ -719,6 +719,15 @@
           if (listen_local == 1)
           {
                logger(LOG_INFO, "forcing socket to listen on local address");
+               /* Really do what the comment above says.  "to" still holds the
+                  destination address of the client's request, which for a
+                  client that broadcasts its read request (Sun boot PROMs do)
+                  is 255.255.255.255.  Binding the data socket to that address
+                  makes every reply unanswerable.  Bind to the wildcard address
+                  instead and let the kernel pick the right source per route. */
+               memset(&to, 0, sizeof(to));
+               to.ss_family = data->client_info->client.ss_family;
+               sockaddr_set_port(&to, 0);
                if (setsockopt(data->sockfd, SOL_SOCKET, SO_BROADCAST, &on, sizeof(on)) != 0) {
                   logger(LOG_ERR, "setsockopt: %s", strerror(errno));
                }
